{
  "requirement": "Plain text defines no attachments. The file root and each `line` item have\nempty attachment lists, including lines that begin with comment delimiters\nfrom another language.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject `requirements.sdk.sources.plain-text.attachments` has one obligation. The file root and each `line` item must have empty attachment lists, including lines that start with another language's comment delimiters.\n\n`crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[bundled_languages_classify_only_their_attachment_syntax]` carries a `{test}` reference to the requirement. Its `test.txt` case parses `\"# just text\\n\"`, which is a line starting with a comment delimiter from another language (Python, TOML or YAML).\n\nThe test collects attachments with `outline().items.iter().flat_map(|i| &i.attachments)`. The items include the root, because `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/source_defaults.rs#fn[built_in_languages_and_structural_search_use_the_project_scope]` notes that a two-line file gives root plus two lines. So the assertion `found == vec![]` covers the root and every line item at once. A plain-text implementation that attached `#` as a Comment, or put an attachment on the root, would fail it.\n\nThe test covers only one line and only the `#` delimiter. The requirement does not distinguish delimiter styles, so I don't count the missing `//`, `/*` and `<!--` cases as a gap. The test also runs `test.txt` through the extension-based plain-text fallback, which is the intended path.",
  "references": "1ef306a66d16e7b2dae06e3da1cb46df121252836c2196dd6eda170dff068010",
